<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_closed_loop_control_systems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Closed-Loop Control Systems</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>A closed-loop control system continuously measures the current output with sensors and compares it to a desired setpoint. The difference between desired and actual state is the error, and control commands are updated in real time to reduce that error. This feedback process allows robots to adapt to disturbances while operating.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_closed_loop_examples">
    <title>Real-World Closed-Loop Examples</title>
    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_cruise_control">
      <title>Automobile Cruise Control</title>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Sense:</em> Wheel-speed sensors estimate current velocity.</p>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Plan:</em> Controller computes speed error relative to the driver setpoint.</p>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Act:</em> Throttle and braking are adjusted to hold target speed across hills and changing conditions.</p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_drone_altitude">
      <title>Drone Altitude Hold</title>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Sense:</em> Altimeter, barometer, GPS, and IMU estimate height and motion.</p>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Plan:</em> Flight controller computes altitude error from hover target.</p>
      <p><em class="emphasis">Act:</em> Motor outputs are adjusted to restore altitude after wind or load disturbances.</p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_pid_foundations">
    <title>PID Control Foundations</title>
    <p>Many robotic controllers are based on PID control. At a minimum, proportional control maps error to actuation with $u=K_p e$, where $K_p$ is proportional gain and $e$ is current error.</p>
    <p>As the robot approaches the target, error decreases and the command naturally reduces, which often improves smoothness. If $K_p$ is too low, the controller may fail to overcome friction near the goal, producing steady-state error (the system settles near, but not at, the target).</p>
    <p>Integral and derivative terms are added in full PID designs to reduce steady-state error and improve damping and stability.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_closed_loop_checks">
    <title>Concept Checks</title>
    <ol>
      <li><p>If a robot continuously uses encoder feedback to correct distance while driving to a target, the system is closed-loop.</p></li>
      <li><p>In proportional control, compute error first, then multiply by gain to obtain output.</p></li>
      <li><p>Engineers use PID over P-only control to improve stability and reduce persistent tracking error.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>
</section>
